            "output": "<path>/workspace/docs/source/sharing_pipeline.rst</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ***********************\n2: Sharing a Rail Pipeline\n3: ***********************\n4: \n5: Here is an example of the first part of the ``goldenspike`` pipeline definition.\n6: \n7: \n8: \n9: .. code-block:: python\n10: \n11:    class GoldenspikePipeline(RailPipeline):\n12: \n13:        def __init__(self):\n14:            RailPipeline.__init__(self)\n15: \n16:            DS = RailStage.data_store\n17:            DS.__class__.allow_overwrite = True\n18:            bands = ['u','g','r','i','z','y']\n19:            band_dict = {band:f'mag_{band}_lsst' for band in bands}\n20:            rename_dict = {f'mag_{band}_lsst_err':f'mag_err_{band}_lsst' for band in bands}\n21: \n22:            self.flow_engine_train = FlowEngine.build(\n23:                flow=flow_file,\n24:                n_samples=50,\n25:                seed=1235,\n26:                output=os.path.join(namer.get_data_dir(DataType.catalog, CatalogType.created), \"output_flow_engine_train.pq\"),\n27:            )\n28: \n29:            self.lsst_error_model_train = LSSTErrorModel.build(\n30:                connections=dict(input=self.flow_engine_train.io.output),   \n31:                bandNames=band_dict, seed=29,\n32:                output=os.path.join(namer.get_data_dir(DataType.catalog, CatalogType.degraded), \"output_lsst_error_model_train.pq\"),\n33:            )\n34: \n35:            self.inv_redshift = InvRedshiftIncompleteness.build(\n36:                connections=dict(input=self.lsst_error_model_train.io.output),\n37:                pivot_redshift=1.0,\n38:                output=os.path.join(namer.get_data_dir(DataType.catalog, CatalogType.degraded), \"output_inv_redshift.pq\"),\n39:            )\n40: \n41:            self.line_confusion = LineConfusion.build(\n42:                connections=dict(input=self.inv_redshift.io.output),\n43:                true_wavelen=5007., wrong_wavelen=3727., frac_wrong=0.05,\n44:                output=os.path.join(namer.get_data_dir(DataType.catalog, CatalogType.degraded), \"output_line_confusion.pq\"),\n45:            )\n46: \n47: What this is doing is:\n48: \n49: #. Defining a class ``GoldenspikePipeline`` to encapsulate the pipeline and setting up that pipeline.\n50: \n51: #. Set up the rail ``DataStore`` for interactive use, allowing you to overwrite output files, (say if you re-run the pipeline in a notebook cell).\n52: \n53: #. Defining some common parameters, e.g., ``bands``, ``bands_dict`` for the pipeline.\n54: \n55: #. Defining four stages, and adding them to the pipeline, note that for each stage the syntax is more or less the same.  We have to define,\n56: \n57:    * The name of the stage, i.e., ``self.flow_engine_train`` will make a stage called ``flow_engine_train`` through some python cleverness.\n58: \n59:    * The class of the stage, which is specified by which type of stage we ask to build, ``FlowEngine.build`` will make a ``FlowEngine`` stage.\n60: \n61:    * Any configuration parameters, which are specified as keyword arguments, e.g., ``n_samples=50``.\n62: \n63:    * Any input connections from other stages, e.g., ``connections=dict(input=self.flow_engine_train.io.output)``,\n64:      in the ``self.lsst_error_model_train`` block will connect the ``output`` of ``self.flow_engine_train``\n65:      to the ``input`` of ``self.lsst_error_model_train``.  Later in that example we\n66:      can see how to connect multiple inputs, e.g., one named ``input`` and \n67:      another named ``model``, as required for an estimator stage.\n68: \n69:    * We use the ``namer`` class and enumerations to ensure that the data end up following our location conventions.\n70: \n\n(End of file - total 70 lines)\n</content>",
